{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem asks to compare a large service with strictly MORE total internal redundancy against a decentralized network. SRC_001 asserts that increased redundancy increases catastrophic correlated failure via the coordination plane (Correlation Horizon), but its mechanism assumes the large service uses a CENTRALIZED coordination plane (consensus/load-balancing logic shared across all nodes). A large service with strictly more total redundancy could use partitioned, sharded, or independently-federated coordination planes with isolated blast radii—strictly more redundancy than the decentralized baseline without a single shared consensus algorithm. The argument never establishes why strictly-more-redundant architectures must concentrate coordination into one shared-logic plane; it silently assumes centralized coordination, then derives the trap from that assumption rather than from the redundancy comparison the problem specifies.","target":"2efe6f67ec62b1cbb9459a3ee1b450e6e117470952f6d1af9fc246c46fe5a36f"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's mechanism only addresses administrative/human (configuration, deployment) correlated failures and treats independent operators using different schedules/tools as sufficient to eliminate them. But the problem explicitly requires distinguishing failures that are TRULY independent from failures that merely APPEAR uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. The mechanism provides no causal basis for asserting operator independence is genuine rather than apparent: independent operators can independently converge on the same flawed configuration (same industry defaults, same upstream library bug, same TLS or DNS setting) without any shared deployment platform or central registry, producing a correlated outage that the given counterconditions do not catch. The counterconditions are limited to a shared managed service or shared config/registry, so they fail to screen common-cause convergence that arises through homogeneous knowledge rather than shared infrastructure. Thus the causal claim outstrips the evidence-distinguishing mechanism the problem demands.","target":"84c2511f8a4666599e27ecdecbd469599a293bb0604beca2dffc6d5cdb52be57"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 reduces resilience to implementation heterogeneity (different languages/OSs preventing a single exploit from causing correlated crash), but its causal mechanism conflates vulnerability heterogeneity with independence of the failure-triggering set. The problem asks to distinguish truly independent failures from merely-appearing-uncorrelated failures with a shared cause. Implementation heterogeneity blocks only exploits specific to a language/OS; it provides no causal mechanism for non-implementation shared causes such as a common data schema, common protocol semantics, common upstream dependency, or a common logical/architectural flaw in the service itself—which is exactly the second countercondition SRC_002 lists. Under those causes, implementation-diverse services can fail concurrently via the same logical path, so heterogeneity yields apparent-but-not-true independence. The mechanism therefore does not establish the independence it claims; it identifies only one narrow channel (exploit portability) and ignores shared-cause channels that heterogeneous implementations do not decouple.","target":"bcbee4028623786e1e83ee2e9187be4db51cef5ebc1ceed92847abc2692ec0a2"}]}